      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U11", "U6", "S2"],
      "pins": ["U11.44", "U6.6", "S2.1", "S2.2"],
      "nets": ["NET0049", "NET0012"],
      "observed": "On the SkylabModule sheet the global label NET0049 is attached both to U11 pin 44 (HW_RESET) and to U6 (BBQ10KBD) pin 6 (ROW1), so the module's hardware-reset line is merged with keyboard matrix row 1. Meanwhile the slide switch S2 (1825232-1) is wired between NET0012 and GND, and NET0012 is U11 pin 48 (GPIO0), not HW_RESET.",
      "expected": "Per the specification, S1 is wired as the HW_RESET switch (pin1->GND, pin2->HW_RESET) and the 7 keyboard row lines go to separate GPIOs; HW_RESET must be its own net driven only by the reset switch, not shared with a keyboard row.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "SkylabModule.kicad_sch: U11 pin '44 HW_RESET' -> label NET0049; U6 BBQ10KBD 'ROW1' pin 6 -> label NET0049; U11 pin '48 GPIO0' -> NET0012; EthernetAndPower.kicad_sch: S2 1825232-1 between NET0012 and GND"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "S1 ... wired as HW_RESET switch ... netlist: pin1->GND, pin2->HW_RESET (SKW92A pin 45) ... netlist shows 5 column + 7 row lines wired directly to SKW92A GPIO pins"}
      ],
      "impact": "The physical reset switch does not reset the module (it only pulls GPIO0 low), and the HW_RESET pin is permanently tied to a scanned keyboard row, so matrix scanning/key presses can spuriously assert or load the reset line, causing unreliable boot/reset behaviour.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing supports one connectivity anomaly: the HW_RESET net (NET0049) is shorted to keyboard matrix row 1, while the reset switch S2 is instead wired to GPIO0 (NET0012)/GND, deviating from the spec's intended dedicated HW_RESET switch."
}

